          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The phrase identifies Jesus’ grave, tomb, or more broadly the realm/state of the dead during the interval before his resurrection.\",\"book_summary\":\"The phrase identifies Jerusalem as the symbolic navel and heart of the land.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Jesus’ place or state of burial/death.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Jerusalem, understood as the geographical-symbolic center of the land.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"This is not merely a dispute over whether the referent includes the tomb, Hades, or the wider passion period. The book replaces the death/burial referent with a city or land-centered referent, likely extending the saying to Jesus’ presence or custody in Jerusalem.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In Matthew 12:40, the same phrase cannot primarily denote both Jesus’ grave or realm of the dead and Jerusalem in the same contextual sense. Accepting the book’s identification requires rejecting the consensus identification as the direct referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s reading is divergent rather than novel because the blind consensus explicitly recognizes Jerusalem or the heart of the land as a minority or rare alternative. Nevertheless, it materially replaces the settled core identification rather than refining its boundaries.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Ezekiel 5:5 and 38:12 support Jerusalem or its people as situated amid the nations or land, but the printed excerpts do not call Jerusalem the “heart” or “navel,” nor do they establish that Matthew 12:40 uses “heart of the earth” in that geographical sense. The immediate Jonah analogy more directly supports confinement associated with death and deliverance.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
